WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
The Central Intelligence Agency and its history of covert operations is dark.

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:10.000
It includes toppling democratic governments in Guatemala, Iran, and Chile,

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:14.880
working with death squads and dictators, mining the harbors of Nicaragua,

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:19.440
and funding groups like the Contras, who deliberately attacked civilian targets

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.240
like adult literacy centers and health care clinics in order to overthrow

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.160
the Sandinista government. One of the most controversial chapters in this

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:33.440
CIA's history remains its post-911 program of renditions and tortures.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.200
Individuals were abducted and subjected to shocking torture,

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.720
sometimes not even people with any nexus to terrorism.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:46.400
Well, urgently, CIA assets and U.S. client states have engaged in torture in the past,

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:52.000
with this program, the CIA, and its contractors carried out the torture itself.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:56.720
And these were not rogue agents acting. These techniques were approved by the president

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:00.880
himself. It was systematic. When the U.S. Senate Intelligence Committee

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.280
tried to exercise its oversight powers and investigate the torture,

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:11.440
the CIA spied on their staffers. Much of that report remains classified by the executive branch

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:17.440
over the objection of U.S. senators. Obama refused to prosecute CIA war criminals,

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:22.560
saying we had to look forward, not backwards. But his administration did oversee the

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:27.360
prosecution with one individual, with a connection to the Bush-era torture program.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.040
That person was whistleblower John Carriacou, who went on ABC News to confirm to the nation

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:37.760
that the U.S. had engaged in waterboarding. Under the Bush administration, when this happened,

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:42.000
the FBI investigated, but ultimately did not prosecute Carriacou.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:47.920
But Obama's policy of looking forwards, not backwards, applied only to torturers.

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:52.800
It did not apply to whistleblowers, and his administration brought indictments against John

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:59.040
Carriacou, including three counts under the Espionage Act. To date, John remains the only

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:03.760
person to have gone to prison in connection with the illegal torture program, and he is our guest

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:09.600
today on Primary Sources. I am Chip Gibbons, Policy Director of Defending Rights in Descent,

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:13.840
an organization which defends your right to know and your freedom to act.

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.040
Defending Rights in Descent, as part of that mission, produces this podcast, which explores

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:25.120
the problems faced by whistleblowers and other truth-tellers who expose civil liberties and

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:29.200
human rights abuses committed under the guise of U.S. national security.

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.200
In a minute, I'm going to bring you John Carriacou. But first, I want to encourage

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:37.920
you to subscribe to the Primary Sources podcast, wherever you're listening to it now,

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:44.000
or visit us on the web at primarysourcespodcast.org. This way, you'll never miss an episode.

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:27.150
Today, we are talking to a CIA whistleblower, John Carriacou. John is a former CIA analyst

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:31.950
and case officer, former senior investigator for the Senate Foreign Relations Committee,

00:03:31.950 --> 00:03:37.150
and former counterterrorism consultant. He is the author of the book The Reluctant Spy,

00:03:37.150 --> 00:03:42.350
My Secret Life and the CIA's Warrant Era. He was the first person to publicly confirm

00:03:42.350 --> 00:03:48.190
that the CIA was engaged in waterboarding. To this day, he remains the only person to go to prison

00:03:48.190 --> 00:03:53.790
in connection with the CIA torture program. To be clear, John was never trained in,

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:58.670
authorized, or engaged in waterboarding. He was imprisoned for blowing the whistle.

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:02.030
John, thank you so much for joining us on Primary Sources.

00:04:02.030 --> 00:04:04.030
Happy to be here, Chip. Thanks for having me.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:08.830
I want to start at the beginning. How did you come to work at the CIA?

00:04:08.830 --> 00:04:14.750
I was in graduate school at George Washington University, working on a master's degree in

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:18.750
legislative affairs. I thought maybe I would make my career on Capitol Hill.

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:24.430
I was taking a class called the Psychology of Leadership, in which we were studying

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:30.670
how foreign leaders made decisions. We were tasked by the professor,

00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:37.310
who was an eminent psychiatrist by the name of Dr. Gerald Post. He had a PhD in political science,

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:45.870
a PhD in psychology, and an MD. He tasked us with shadowing our bosses for a week and then

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:51.310
writing a psychological profile on them. I was working at a labor union at the time,

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:56.190
the United Food and Commercial Workers Union. My boss was one of these old-school,

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:04.830
tough guy union organizers. He was rough, he was mean, and I was a little bit afraid of him.

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:10.110
Well, halfway through the week, we got into an argument and I called him a racist, which he was.

00:05:10.750 --> 00:05:17.470
And he balled up his fists and said a stance, and I put up my hands to defend myself,

00:05:17.470 --> 00:05:22.670
and he says to me, my penis is bigger than yours. And I said, what? I said,

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:29.070
you're crazy, and I quit. And I walked out. So I wrote this in the paper, and I used other

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:34.350
examples, and I said that I thought he was a sociopath with psychopathic and possibly violent

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:39.950
tendencies. And so a week later, I got the paper back from Dr. Post, and he gave me an A,

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:45.550
and he wrote in the margin, please see me after class. So I went after class to his

00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:50.990
office, and I said, you wanted to see me. And he said, look, I'm not really a professor

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:57.230
here. I am the CIA officer undercover as a professor here. And I'm looking for people

00:05:57.230 --> 00:06:03.150
who might fit into the CIA's culture. I think you would be a good CIA officer. Are you interested?

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:07.310
And I really didn't have anything else going on. Always wanted to see the world.

00:06:07.310 --> 00:06:12.350
I was going to get married in like six weeks and had no job. So next thing I knew, I was in the

00:06:12.350 --> 00:06:18.350
CIA. And what's it like when you first start working there? Wow. It was, you know,

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:24.430
I would park my car out in the North 40 and I would walk all the way around the compound

00:06:24.430 --> 00:06:29.470
so I could come in the main entrance and walk across the seal and see the Wall of Honor and,

00:06:29.470 --> 00:06:35.790
you know, the flags and the portraits of the directors. It was so exciting. I couldn't wait

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:40.670
to get to work in the morning. And that lasted for many years where I literally could

00:06:40.670 --> 00:06:44.830
not wait to get to work in the morning. I would go in on Saturdays just because

00:06:44.830 --> 00:06:49.310
it was so much fun. You know, I couldn't wait to see what was going on in the world

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:56.270
that wasn't being covered in the newspapers. And what year is this? I joined January 1990.

00:06:56.270 --> 00:07:01.230
And you've mentioned before some of your public talks that early on, I believe it was your boss

00:07:01.230 --> 00:07:05.870
or some other mentor gives you advice about not doing anything you wouldn't want to read

00:07:05.870 --> 00:07:09.950
on the front page of the Washington Post. Is that right? Yeah, that was my very first

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:13.710
week on the job. And it really did stick with me. And another thing that stuck with me

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:18.350
from that week was the oath. You know, we all stood up and we put our right hands in the air

00:07:18.350 --> 00:07:23.470
and we swore to protect and defend the Constitution of the United States against all enemies foreign

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:29.390
and domestic. And I took that oath very seriously. So we talked a bit about your initial reactions

00:07:29.390 --> 00:07:35.950
to the say, what's your life like at the CIA before 9-11? 4-9-11 was a lot of fun.

00:07:37.070 --> 00:07:41.630
Not just because the work was interesting to me. I had a bachelor's degree in Middle Eastern

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:44.990
studies. And of course, I was working on the Middle East. But, you know, during the Clinton

00:07:44.990 --> 00:07:51.710
administration, President Clinton was very serious about human rights. And he ordered

00:07:51.710 --> 00:07:57.710
something called a cull, where the CIA was mandated with going through its files and firing

00:07:58.270 --> 00:08:03.950
literally every recruited asset, every source who had a human rights problem in his or her

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:10.030
background. During the Reagan and HW Bush administrations, the CIA was recruiting

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:16.670
people who had murdered nuns in Central America or who had, you know, been on death squads or

00:08:16.670 --> 00:08:22.670
tortured indigenous people. And we got rid of all of those people. And in the beginning,

00:08:23.550 --> 00:08:29.630
you know, I wasn't sure if I should take that seriously. And then I actually watched it unfold.

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:34.110
And I thought, wow, they're really serious about this. That was laudable. And that policy

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:40.430
remained until 9-11. And the other thing Clinton does that I will give him credit for,

00:08:40.430 --> 00:08:46.350
loathe to give Bill Clinton credit, but I will do so. In this case, he by executive order orders

00:08:46.350 --> 00:08:51.630
such a classification of a lot of information about U.S. activities in Central and South America,

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:57.230
I believe, Chile, Argentina. I don't recall Guatemala as one of them or not. But thanks

00:08:57.230 --> 00:09:03.790
to these executive orders, they declassify huge swaths of some of the worst U.S. policies

00:09:03.790 --> 00:09:08.270
in Latin America. How do people in the agency react to that? I'm curious.

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:14.830
You know, it's sort of an administrative thing that happens every once in a while. So people don't

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:20.030
really pay any attention. And the ones that do just do as they're told. That information can

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:26.670
now be found at the National Security Archives at George Washington University and also online

00:09:26.670 --> 00:09:32.030
at the Clinton Presidential Library. So now it's available for scholars, any scholars who

00:09:32.030 --> 00:09:36.830
want to study what used to be classified. But you raise a very important point here, Chip. And

00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:42.750
that's the over declassification. I'm sorry, it's the over classification of information.

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:50.110
We have billions and billions of pages of classified documents that ought not be classified.

00:09:50.110 --> 00:09:55.790
And while we have a mandatory declassification law in the United States after 30 years,

00:09:55.790 --> 00:10:01.710
it's not automatic. A human being has to actually go through it to make sure

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:06.830
that the operations are not still ongoing and that the sources are dead.

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:11.310
Yeah. I mean, some of the stuff they classify is outrageous. I'm working on a book right now on

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:16.030
the FBI. So I'm getting a bunch of files and they sent me something and in response to

00:10:16.030 --> 00:10:21.550
my FOIA request, it was declassified. You could see the date like June 17th, 2021,

00:10:21.630 --> 00:10:26.590
declassified. And it had previously been classified under the Foreign Counterintelligence Authority.

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:31.870
So very, very serious, right? Not a single redaction. I showed this to someone that used to work in

00:10:31.870 --> 00:10:36.510
intelligence. They were surprised they released without a single redaction. A group of anti-war

00:10:36.510 --> 00:10:42.830
protesters went to a federal building and protested the Gulf War. And this was classified for 20,

00:10:42.830 --> 00:10:48.750
30 years. It is entirety as foreign counterintelligence because some of the protesters,

00:10:49.310 --> 00:10:54.110
this is in the 90s, not in the Hoover era, may have been members of the Communist Party.

00:10:54.110 --> 00:10:59.870
Thus it counted as Soviet active measures in the United States. And this is 90s, right? We know

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:04.750
that sort of thing went on in the 50s and the 40s and the 60s. But it's just like, this is a real

00:11:04.750 --> 00:11:09.230
great use of classification authorities. Well, you know, when I joined the CIA, I also had to

00:11:09.230 --> 00:11:13.150
put my right hand in the air and swear that I had never been a member of the Communist Party

00:11:13.230 --> 00:11:17.950
or of any organization associated with the Communist Party. And I laughed and I said,

00:11:17.950 --> 00:11:21.550
seriously? And the guy's like, yeah, it's just never been updated.

00:11:21.550 --> 00:11:25.550
You're also in your sort of pre-911 time, you're in Greece for a while.

00:11:25.550 --> 00:11:32.830
I was. I was in Greece from 1998 to 2000. I got bored after seven years in analysis. And I

00:11:32.830 --> 00:11:38.110
made a very unusual switch to counterterrorism operations. And because I was literally

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:44.830
the only person in the entire CIA who spoke both Greek and Arabic, I was sent to Greece to work on

00:11:44.830 --> 00:11:50.670
counterterrorism issues from 1998 to 2000, which became kind of hairy for me.

00:11:50.670 --> 00:11:55.470
Yeah. Many people may not think of Greece as a particular hotspot, but they have killed CIA

00:11:55.470 --> 00:12:02.430
officers in Greece. Yeah. Before 2002, there was a group called Revolutionary Organization

00:12:02.510 --> 00:12:13.070
17 November, a stridently anti-American, anti-NATO, anti-Turkey. And it was violently

00:12:13.070 --> 00:12:20.910
Stalinist in its ideology. It was finally broken in March of 2002, but it started off by murdering

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:27.630
the CIA station chief in Athens in December 1975 and killed two U.S. defense attachés,

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:35.230
a U.S. Air Force technical sergeant, the Turkish ambassador, the Turkish deputy ambassador,

00:12:35.230 --> 00:12:41.710
the British defense attaché. I mean, they ended up killing 28 people over the years,

00:12:41.710 --> 00:12:46.670
and not only had they not been caught, none of them had ever even been identified.

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:48.830
And so that was my job to work against them.

00:12:48.830 --> 00:12:51.470
Did you ever fear for your life when you were in Greece?

00:12:51.470 --> 00:12:57.150
Yeah, I did. We spent more money on security in Athens than we spent in Beirut,

00:12:57.150 --> 00:13:00.910
and you know how Beirut was in the 70s, 80s, and 90s.

00:13:00.910 --> 00:13:02.590
I was not there, but I've heard.

00:13:03.710 --> 00:13:11.470
So I was very, very careful with my personal safety. And then near the end of my tour,

00:13:11.470 --> 00:13:16.110
a British military officer, Brigadier General Stephen Saunders, moved in

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:22.830
caddy corner from me, and we became friendly. And he was jokingly mocking me at a dinner party one

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:30.510
night. He was making fun of my car. I had a fully armored BMW 540, and he said, you Americans,

00:13:30.510 --> 00:13:36.830
you're so paranoid about security. He said, this is a NATO country. It's an EU country.

00:13:36.830 --> 00:13:42.350
What are you so afraid of? And everybody chuckled. And I said, you Brits live

00:13:42.350 --> 00:13:46.190
in a dream world, if you think because they have palm trees in pretty beaches,

00:13:46.190 --> 00:13:49.550
they're not going to kill you if they have an opportunity. If they can get to you,

00:13:49.550 --> 00:13:53.790
they're going to kill you. And everybody laughed. And two weeks later, they killed him.

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:54.110
Wow.

00:13:54.110 --> 00:13:57.790
And they killed him right in the street on the way to the British Embassy. Well,

00:13:57.790 --> 00:14:02.350
I was behind him by about a quarter of a mile. When I inched my way up to his car,

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:08.510
I recognized his car. There was blood everywhere. And after he had been shot,

00:14:08.510 --> 00:14:15.390
they shot him with an anti-tank round. They blew his right hand off and then shot him three times

00:14:15.390 --> 00:14:21.070
in the chest and the stomach with a 45. A cab driver pulled him out and put him in his cab and rushed

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:25.790
him to a hospital. But he died just a few minutes after he got there. As if that wasn't bad enough,

00:14:26.350 --> 00:14:32.590
what 17 November used to do is they used to throw a manifesto at the site of their hits.

00:14:33.070 --> 00:14:35.310
And they threw a manifesto. And they said,

00:14:35.310 --> 00:14:43.870
ida meto negalo catascopos. We saw the big spy, but he was in an armored car and he was wearing

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:50.110
a weapon. And my boss called me and he said, this manifesto, this is about you.

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:56.510
And I said, nonsense. I said, I've been so careful with my security. I would leave my house

00:14:56.510 --> 00:14:59.950
a different time every day. I would take a different route to work every day,

00:15:00.030 --> 00:15:06.030
but they were looking to kill me that day. And it said in the manifesto, we decided to

00:15:06.030 --> 00:15:10.990
carry out the revolutionary sentence on the war criminal Saunders. And so they killed Steve

00:15:10.990 --> 00:15:16.430
Saunders instead. So two hours later, man, I was at the airport and security was picking up my

00:15:16.430 --> 00:15:20.270
family at the house and brought them to the airport. And we got the first Delta flight

00:15:20.270 --> 00:15:25.630
back to New York. Where are you on September 11, 2001? I had an appointment that morning

00:15:25.630 --> 00:15:31.310
with Condi Rice. I was supposed to go to the White House with Cofer Black, who was the head of

00:15:31.310 --> 00:15:38.190
the CIS Counterterrorism Center. It's very quaint now, but the government printing office was going

00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:50.030
to publish a volume of cables from 1947 to 1969. And several of those cables mentioned sources

00:15:50.030 --> 00:15:56.670
of ours who were still alive. And so we wanted to ask Condi to hold off on printing the cables.

00:15:57.230 --> 00:16:02.990
So I got a call that my car was ready to take us to the White House. I walked over to Cofer's

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:10.030
office to tell him that we were ready. And his secretary had a TV on her desk and it was on.

00:16:10.030 --> 00:16:13.470
And the World Trade Center was burning. I said, what happened to the World Trade Center?

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:17.150
And she said a plane flew into it. And I said, because I was clueless,

00:16:17.150 --> 00:16:23.630
I said, that happened once before. In 1930, a plane flew into the Empire State Building,

00:16:23.630 --> 00:16:28.030
but it was really foggy and rainy that day. And it's so clear today. How can you not see

00:16:28.030 --> 00:16:33.630
that you're flying into the World Trade Center? And as soon as I said it, the second plane hit

00:16:33.630 --> 00:16:39.310
the second tower. And then she turns to me. She was sitting. She turns to me and she says,

00:16:39.310 --> 00:16:44.670
did you see that or did I imagine it? And I ran back to my office and I said, guys,

00:16:44.670 --> 00:16:48.270
two planes just hit both towers of the World Trade Center. I think we're under attack.

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:54.990
So everybody went into the big bullpen where Cofer's office was. There were probably 150

00:16:54.990 --> 00:17:00.670
cubicles and desks in there. And it was just silence. We had TVs on every network.

00:17:00.670 --> 00:17:06.670
And then finally someone shouted, well, somebody please lead. And it was like snapped Cofer

00:17:06.670 --> 00:17:12.030
out of out of his shock at you, go to security, you go to the director's office,

00:17:12.030 --> 00:17:18.350
you go here, go there. And we all just stood there and watched it. And then the Pentagon

00:17:18.350 --> 00:17:22.910
was hit. And then there were these rumors that a car bomb had gone off at the State Department.

00:17:22.910 --> 00:17:28.350
And then finally a security officer came in and shouted, everybody evacuate right now.

00:17:28.830 --> 00:17:33.710
And nobody moved. 20 minutes later, he comes back and he says, if you don't evacuate,

00:17:33.710 --> 00:17:40.270
you'll be arrested. So we're like, okay, everybody evacuates. It took me two hours

00:17:40.270 --> 00:17:46.510
to get out of the CIA parking lot, if you can imagine. I got about halfway home. I lived in

00:17:46.510 --> 00:17:52.590
Arlington, Virginia in courthouse, it's called at the time. And I couldn't get anywhere near my

00:17:52.590 --> 00:17:57.790
apartment because they closed off all the roads. People were streaming out of Washington.

00:17:57.790 --> 00:18:02.270
So I just abandoned my car on the George Washington Parkway and I walked the rest of

00:18:02.270 --> 00:18:08.030
the way. My then girlfriend, she became my wife, met up with me at my place. We walked

00:18:08.030 --> 00:18:13.390
around looking for someplace to give blood and couldn't find any place because the lines

00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:17.150
were so long. And finally I said, this is ridiculous. We need to get back to work.

00:18:17.150 --> 00:18:22.270
So we walked back to my car. We drove back to headquarters. She was also a CIA officer.

00:18:22.270 --> 00:18:26.990
And then I didn't leave for the next four days. I just slept under my desk and worked

00:18:26.990 --> 00:18:31.950
pretty much 24 hours a day. And what becomes your job at the CIA after 9-11?

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:39.230
In the immediate aftermath, hours, days, even weeks, it was all name traces. Every officer,

00:18:39.230 --> 00:18:46.350
every CIA officer around the world was sending in name trace requests. Who is Muhammad Abdullah?

00:18:46.350 --> 00:18:53.630
Who is Rashid Muhammad? Because people were ratting each other out. People were volunteering to

00:18:53.630 --> 00:18:59.550
help. So we were investigating everybody. And then we started sending teams over to

00:19:00.190 --> 00:19:04.830
Afghanistan. And I went to the deputy director of counterterrorism and I said,

00:19:04.830 --> 00:19:11.310
look, my Arabic is outstanding. You've got to send me. And I volunteered three times.

00:19:11.310 --> 00:19:17.550
And three times they blew me off. Well, I ran into an old buddy of mine. He was a contractor.

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:24.430
He's the second most wounded soldier in American history, Billy Wah. He has 17

00:19:24.430 --> 00:19:31.550
purple hearts. He just died two years ago. Yeah. Fought in Korea, Vietnam, Grenada,

00:19:31.550 --> 00:19:38.030
all the way to Panama. So I ran into Billy in the hall. And I said, hey, man, where have you been?

00:19:38.030 --> 00:19:41.310
And he kind of looks around and he says, Afghanistan. I said, really? What are you

00:19:41.310 --> 00:19:46.750
doing in Afghanistan? This is October of 2001. And he says, I've been killing people. What

00:19:46.750 --> 00:19:52.190
do you think I've been doing? And I said, that's why they haven't sent me. They didn't

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:57.870
need a translator. They weren't interrogating anybody. They were just killing them.

00:19:57.870 --> 00:20:03.230
And so finally I went into the deputy director's office with this totally idle threat. And I said,

00:20:03.230 --> 00:20:09.710
if you don't send me to Afghanistan right now, I am going straight to Exxon with my Arabic.

00:20:09.710 --> 00:20:13.790
And I am never looking back. And he said, all right, all right, take it easy.

00:20:13.790 --> 00:20:18.510
Can you go to Pakistan? I said, yes, when tomorrow? I said, yes. What do you want

00:20:18.510 --> 00:20:23.710
me to do? He said, I want you to be chief of counterterrorism operations. I said, done.

00:20:23.710 --> 00:20:28.190
I'm going to go home right now and pack. And so I did. I called my girlfriend. I said,

00:20:28.190 --> 00:20:31.790
hey, I got to go to Pakistan tomorrow. I don't know when I'm going to come back.

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:35.390
She said, okay, I'll meet you at your apartment. I'll help you pack. And she did.

00:20:35.390 --> 00:20:37.950
She took me to the airport and I went to Pakistan.

00:20:37.950 --> 00:20:43.230
And this brings us to Abu Zubedah. Who did the CIA think he was? I believe some of

00:20:43.230 --> 00:20:46.190
who they initially thought he was has been called into question.

00:20:46.190 --> 00:20:49.870
Oh, it's been proven incorrect. Yes, correct. We explained to listeners

00:20:49.870 --> 00:20:54.110
who might not have been alive 20 years ago when he was captured.

00:20:54.110 --> 00:20:58.510
You know, and it's funny. In my mind, this was all yesterday. It's hard to.

00:20:59.150 --> 00:21:04.190
I have to remind myself that 20 years have passed, which is crazy to me.

00:21:04.190 --> 00:21:10.750
Abu Zubedah, we thought at the time was the number three in al-Qaeda after

00:21:10.750 --> 00:21:15.950
Osama bin Laden and Ayman as a Wahdi. Abu Zubedah was not the number three in al-Qaeda.

00:21:15.950 --> 00:21:22.030
He was actually never a member of al-Qaeda. He was a very bad guy, a very bad and dangerous man.

00:21:22.590 --> 00:21:27.870
He was the one who had founded, established al-Qaeda's two training camps in southern

00:21:27.870 --> 00:21:34.270
Afghanistan. He founded the House of Martyrs al-Qaeda safe house in Peshawar, Pakistan.

00:21:34.270 --> 00:21:38.990
And he was a logistics guy. So if you wanted to go to Afghanistan to fight,

00:21:38.990 --> 00:21:42.990
you talked to Abu Zubedah. If you were tired of the fight and you wanted to go home,

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:49.070
Abu Zubedah could get you a fake passport, tickets, smuggle you, whatever. You needed money.

00:21:49.070 --> 00:21:53.710
You saw Abu Zubedah. You needed some kind of assistance for your family. You saw Abu Zubedah.

00:21:53.710 --> 00:21:59.390
So his name kept coming up. And even Time Magazine had identified him as the number

00:21:59.390 --> 00:22:05.230
three in al-Qaeda. So he was a major target for us. I had been in Pakistan

00:22:06.110 --> 00:22:12.030
two weeks. So I was just getting used to my routine. And the routine was 18 hour workdays.

00:22:12.030 --> 00:22:17.630
You go in when it's dark. You leave when it's dark. You just look for al-Qaeda fighters all day long,

00:22:17.630 --> 00:22:23.150
come up with a plan. This was hard work. And I had a staff of nine retired senior

00:22:23.150 --> 00:22:28.430
intelligence service officers. One was the former associate deputy director of the CIA.

00:22:28.430 --> 00:22:33.070
They were all patriots. They all volunteered to come back and they were assigned to

00:22:33.070 --> 00:22:38.590
Pakistan. But because they had green contractor badges and not blue employee badges,

00:22:38.590 --> 00:22:43.150
they couldn't have a leadership position. So I was the boss, even though all of them had,

00:22:44.030 --> 00:22:50.270
I figured one time they had like 260 years of experience combined and had all been in

00:22:50.270 --> 00:22:57.710
the senior intelligence service. So I got a call early one Friday. The weekend in Pakistan

00:22:57.710 --> 00:23:02.270
is Friday and Saturday. And I didn't get the call on a Friday. I got the call on a Saturday

00:23:02.270 --> 00:23:06.830
because Saturday was the only day I allowed myself the luxury of sleeping in. You had to go to work

00:23:06.830 --> 00:23:11.710
seven days a week anyway. So at least I'd sleep till eight on Saturdays. Well, I get this call

00:23:11.710 --> 00:23:17.310
at like six o'clock Saturday morning, come to the office immediately. So I get showered, get

00:23:17.310 --> 00:23:22.590
dressed, rushed to the office. Everybody's there. The station chief, the deputy, the FBI

00:23:22.590 --> 00:23:28.510
legal attache, his deputy, the chief of operations and me, six of us were meeting in the chief's

00:23:28.510 --> 00:23:34.990
office. And he said, we got a cable overnight from our friends at the fort in Maryland. And

00:23:34.990 --> 00:23:40.910
they say that Abu Zubaydah is somewhere in Pakistan. And our orders are to take him alive. And everybody

00:23:40.910 --> 00:23:46.430
looks at me. And I said, what do you mean somewhere in Pakistan? I said, Pakistan's the size of Texas.

00:23:46.430 --> 00:23:51.630
It got 120 million people here. What do you mean? Go catch him. Look seriously. And he said,

00:23:51.630 --> 00:23:57.070
you got to come up with a plan. So this is a very long story. And I'm going to make it

00:23:57.070 --> 00:24:03.310
very, very short. I came up with several different plans, all of which were failures.

00:24:03.310 --> 00:24:08.590
And so finally I said, I need a targeting analyst. The targeting analyst is unlike

00:24:08.590 --> 00:24:12.430
the kind of analyst I was. The kind of analyst I was, you think the big thoughts,

00:24:12.430 --> 00:24:15.870
you write these papers, you send them to the White House, nobody ever reads them.

00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:19.550
And then you tell your friends how important you are. Targeting analysts

00:24:19.550 --> 00:24:25.790
pour through sometimes millions of pieces of data to try to locate somebody

00:24:25.790 --> 00:24:31.470
somewhere in the world. Maybe there's an email, maybe there's a telephone intercept,

00:24:31.470 --> 00:24:37.470
maybe there's like two seconds of a conversation that NSA pulled out of the air somehow,

00:24:37.470 --> 00:24:43.710
and it enables you to pinpoint a location with the result being that you either capture

00:24:43.710 --> 00:24:50.350
or kill that person. So our orders were to capture him. So I called this targeting

00:24:50.350 --> 00:24:53.470
analyst, who was a friend of mine, and I told him, I need for you to come to

00:24:53.470 --> 00:24:59.390
Pakistan like right now. He got there maybe 24 hours later. I picked him up at four o'clock in

00:24:59.390 --> 00:25:04.270
the morning. We went to the office at seven and I told him what we needed. We've got to

00:25:04.270 --> 00:25:09.390
find Abu Zubeda and we have to take him alive. So he started going through the data.

00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:16.190
It took him about two weeks. And finally he said to me, I cannot pinpoint this

00:25:16.190 --> 00:25:22.190
to any fewer than 14 possible locations. I said 14. We had never done more than

00:25:22.190 --> 00:25:26.990
two raids before in a single night where you break down the door at two in the morning and you

00:25:26.990 --> 00:25:32.990
grab everybody. We're going to have to hit 14 places simultaneously. So I sent a cable to

00:25:32.990 --> 00:25:41.790
headquarters and I said, I need a 737. I need 36 guys, half CIA, half FBI, because 9-11 was

00:25:41.790 --> 00:25:46.750
still an open criminal investigation. So the FBI technically had the lead when it came to the

00:25:46.750 --> 00:25:51.150
investigation part. We had the lead when it came to the kicking down the door and

00:25:51.150 --> 00:25:57.310
grabbing people part. I needed pallets of weapons and ammunition. I needed night vision goggles

00:25:57.310 --> 00:26:04.430
and secure communications and battering rams and walkie-talkies and you name it. They loaded it

00:26:04.430 --> 00:26:12.910
all on to an unmarked 737 and it arrived 36 hours later. So we bought two safe houses.

00:26:12.910 --> 00:26:20.110
All of our targets were in Faisalabad and Lahore. And on the night of March 22, 2002,

00:26:20.110 --> 00:26:27.390
we hit all 14 sites simultaneously and in one of them on the second floor. The first floor was full

00:26:27.390 --> 00:26:32.030
of Al Qaeda fighters. They knew that there were VIPs on the second floor, but they didn't know who it

00:26:32.030 --> 00:26:36.830
was because there was a door that was kept locked and the only person who went up there was the

00:26:36.830 --> 00:26:42.510
T-boy who delivered food and tea. And so we started breaking down the door with a battering

00:26:42.510 --> 00:26:48.510
ram, Abu Zubaydah, a Syrian bomber, and Abu Zubaydah's bodyguard all fled to the roof

00:26:48.510 --> 00:26:52.990
and they tried to jump to the roof of the next door building. The bomber was first,

00:26:52.990 --> 00:26:59.630
a Pakistani policeman who I had specifically told, don't shoot, shot.

00:26:59.630 --> 00:27:04.190
Shoot, of course. Yeah. And he killed the Syrian bomber with one shot,

00:27:04.190 --> 00:27:09.870
dead before he hit the ground. The photographs are grisly. Abu Zubaydah jumped second and was

00:27:09.870 --> 00:27:16.270
shot in the stomach, the groin and the thigh. And then the bodyguard jumped third and he

00:27:16.270 --> 00:27:24.110
was shot right through the femur, nearly lost his leg. So we rushed Abu Zubaydah to a local hospital,

00:27:24.110 --> 00:27:29.230
one of the worst places on earth that I've ever been. Word got around the Al Qaeda community

00:27:29.230 --> 00:27:33.550
that we had gotten them and they began driving by the hospital and just opening fire on the

00:27:33.550 --> 00:27:40.110
hospital. Wow. So I said to the Pakistani major that I was with, if they realize how

00:27:40.110 --> 00:27:44.350
lightly armed we are, we're dead. We can't make it out of here alive. Can you get a

00:27:44.350 --> 00:27:48.750
helicopter to land in the parking lot? He said, I think so. So 20 minutes later,

00:27:48.750 --> 00:27:53.870
a helicopter lands in the parking lot and I went into the operating room like this

00:27:53.870 --> 00:27:58.350
and I said, doc, wrap it up. We have to go. They sewed them closed as fast as they could.

00:27:58.350 --> 00:28:02.430
We loaded them on the helicopter and we flew to a Pakistani military base about

00:28:02.430 --> 00:28:08.110
50 miles away. He was in a coma for the first 24 hours. But the analyst called me

00:28:08.110 --> 00:28:11.710
and he said he had spoken to George Tenet, who was the director of the CIA.

00:28:11.710 --> 00:28:19.390
And George said his exact words were 24, 7 CIA eyes on, do not leave his bedside. Well,

00:28:19.390 --> 00:28:24.350
I had already been up for 24 hours, plus 24 hours that Abu Zubaydah was in the coma.

00:28:24.350 --> 00:28:29.470
And I was afraid I'd fall asleep and he would like wake up and run away or the doctor maybe

00:28:29.470 --> 00:28:33.390
was Al Qaeda and the doctor would break him out. So I tore up a sheet and I tied him

00:28:33.390 --> 00:28:39.310
to the bed, wrists and ankles. And then finally after 24 hours, he began to stir.

00:28:39.390 --> 00:28:45.790
And I was wearing a SpongeBob square pants t-shirt that my kids had bought me because it was the only

00:28:45.790 --> 00:28:51.550
clean shirt I had. And one of the FBI guys brought it over for me. And as soon as he opened his eyes,

00:28:51.550 --> 00:28:56.990
tied to the bed like that, he opened his eyes and he saw SpongeBob. His heart just went crazy

00:28:56.990 --> 00:29:01.150
and he coded. And so they had to come in and shock him. And they gave him some

00:29:01.150 --> 00:29:04.830
demoral and he was out again. Finally, he woke up again, I don't know, five,

00:29:04.830 --> 00:29:11.870
six hours later. And he like this, he motioned for me to come next to him. So I moved his oxygen

00:29:11.870 --> 00:29:19.470
mask and I said, what is your name? And he shook his head. So I said, again, and he said in English,

00:29:19.470 --> 00:29:24.030
I will not speak to you in God's language. And I said, that's okay, Abu Zubaydah, we know who

00:29:24.030 --> 00:29:28.750
you are. And he started crying. And he said, please, brother, kill me, take the pillow and

00:29:28.750 --> 00:29:32.830
kill me. And I said, we've been looking for you for a long time. Nobody's going to kill

00:29:32.830 --> 00:29:38.590
you. I said, you're going to get the best medical care that the American government can provide.

00:29:38.590 --> 00:29:45.070
Later on, he was very, very worried, as you can imagine. Later on, he asked what was going

00:29:45.070 --> 00:29:50.430
to happen to him. And I said, I honestly don't know, but I'm going to give you some advice.

00:29:50.430 --> 00:29:56.590
I said, I am the nicest guy that you're going to meet in this experience. My colleagues,

00:29:57.310 --> 00:30:03.230
they're not nice like I am. And if there's one thing that you have to do, it's that you have to

00:30:03.230 --> 00:30:09.630
cooperate. And he said, you seem like a nice man, but you're the enemy, and I'll never cooperate.

00:30:09.630 --> 00:30:15.950
And I said, suit yourself. So over the next, I was with him for 56 hours. I never left his

00:30:15.950 --> 00:30:21.790
bedside for 56 hours. He cried a lot. He said he would never know the touch of a woman.

00:30:21.790 --> 00:30:26.670
He would never know the joy of fatherhood. I said, look, you, you aren't the victim here.

00:30:26.670 --> 00:30:30.750
There were 50,000 people in those towers. What did you think we would do?

00:30:30.750 --> 00:30:35.630
Did you not think we would try to find you and kill you and kill Bin Laden?

00:30:35.630 --> 00:30:40.830
You're lucky to be alive right now. That's why I'm telling you, you have to cooperate.

00:30:40.830 --> 00:30:46.190
The rest of your life can be easy or it can be terrible. It's up to you.

00:30:46.830 --> 00:30:53.870
Finally, the CIA sent in a private jet and three FBI agents, and I carried them out on a gurney.

00:30:53.870 --> 00:30:58.190
He asked me to hold his hand. So I'm holding the gurney with one hand and holding his hand with

00:30:58.190 --> 00:31:03.230
my other. And we maneuvered him onto the plane. We put him on the luggage rack in the back

00:31:03.230 --> 00:31:08.110
of the plane and tied him down. And I leaned over and I said, remember, you have to cooperate.

00:31:08.110 --> 00:31:12.750
And he squeezed my hand. And I said, good luck. And then I never saw him again.

00:31:12.750 --> 00:31:17.550
So we later learned that Abu Zabadeh was waterboarded 83 times.

00:31:18.430 --> 00:31:23.790
When did you first learn the CIA was engaged in waterboarding and these sort of other torture

00:31:23.790 --> 00:31:24.990
techniques update? Yes.

00:31:24.990 --> 00:31:29.870
Euphemistically called enhanced interrogation. I knew that it was coming because when I got

00:31:29.870 --> 00:31:36.510
back to headquarters in May of 2002, I was asked if I wanted to be trained in the use

00:31:36.510 --> 00:31:42.350
of the so-called enhanced interrogation techniques. I had a moral and ethical problem

00:31:42.350 --> 00:31:47.150
with them. I had a legal problem with them. Besides being immoral and unethical, to me,

00:31:47.150 --> 00:31:54.110
they were clearly illegal. We have very clear laws and our signatories to international treaties

00:31:54.110 --> 00:32:00.350
that ban this kind of behavior. So I declined. And I'm sorry to say that of the 14 people

00:32:00.350 --> 00:32:04.110
who were asked to go into training, I was the only one who said no. That was very

00:32:04.110 --> 00:32:09.070
disappointing to me. So I knew that it was coming. And then on August 1st, 2002,

00:32:09.710 --> 00:32:16.510
the CIA began to torture Abu Zabadeh. Now, there's a very important lie involved here.

00:32:16.510 --> 00:32:21.310
This is another very long story that I'm going to make very short. The CIA had two contract

00:32:21.310 --> 00:32:25.870
psychologists, James Mitchell and Bruce Jessen. They were the ones who came up with the

00:32:25.870 --> 00:32:31.070
torture program and sold it to CIA leadership as something that they had studied and they

00:32:31.070 --> 00:32:35.310
had invented and they had proven was successful and was workable.

00:32:35.390 --> 00:32:38.430
Especially it was learned helplessness, right? That was their cycle.

00:32:38.430 --> 00:32:42.510
Learned helplessness. And you know what? Chip, after 20 years, that's still a

00:32:42.510 --> 00:32:45.550
chilling term to me. I still get chills just when I hear it.

00:32:45.550 --> 00:32:51.070
I remember when I was in law school, we read, this is an aside Peter Singer's book on animal

00:32:51.070 --> 00:32:57.150
rights. He goes into like, well, you think animal testing is okay because it's for

00:32:57.150 --> 00:33:00.510
important drugs, like life-saving drugs. He goes, no, most of it are these people

00:33:00.510 --> 00:33:04.990
testing learned helplessness. And he explained the experiments. They were doing

00:33:04.990 --> 00:33:11.710
animals and they're horrifying. And he tells some sort of a story where a visiting

00:33:11.710 --> 00:33:15.070
scientist comes in and looks at what they're doing in a psych lab and says,

00:33:15.070 --> 00:33:18.030
what are you trying to do to these monkeys? And he says, well, we're trying to

00:33:18.030 --> 00:33:21.950
induce psychopathology and this could be a made up story. I don't know.

00:33:21.950 --> 00:33:24.350
And the visiting scientist says, well, I think you have enough

00:33:24.350 --> 00:33:30.190
psychopathology in this lab already. I knew about the torture program

00:33:30.190 --> 00:33:35.390
and it wasn't until years later, I made that connection between these horrifying

00:33:35.390 --> 00:33:41.070
experiments on torturing animals was sort of paved the way for torturing people.

00:33:41.070 --> 00:33:48.270
I believe that. Mitchell and Jessen were paid $108 million for introducing this torture system to

00:33:48.270 --> 00:33:54.670
the CIA. And what was happening was at the secret prison, the FBI was interrogating

00:33:54.670 --> 00:34:00.190
Abuzabeta the way the FBI does it. They establish a rapport. They establish a relationship.

00:34:00.190 --> 00:34:04.510
Here's an orange. Here's a cigarette. Would you like to write a letter to your mother?

00:34:04.510 --> 00:34:10.670
And the subject opens up. It takes time, but it works. And it's worked since the Nuremberg

00:34:10.670 --> 00:34:15.470
trials, right? Which is when they introduced this system. Mitchell and Jessen wanted to prove

00:34:15.470 --> 00:34:20.350
that their method was better and that it would lead to quicker results.

00:34:20.350 --> 00:34:27.710
So the FBI was withdrawn from the secret site. Mitchell and Jessen took over and they immediately

00:34:27.710 --> 00:34:32.590
began torturing Abuzabeta. Now, here's where the big lie comes in. Mitchell and Jessen started

00:34:32.590 --> 00:34:37.630
torturing Abuzabeta and he immediately clammed up, just stopped talking. They almost killed him.

00:34:37.630 --> 00:34:43.470
He had to be revived after being waterboarded. They drowned him and he had to be revived.

00:34:43.470 --> 00:34:51.630
So what they did is they waterboarded him 83 times and then they wrote a cable to headquarters

00:34:51.630 --> 00:34:57.310
saying we waterboarded him once. He cracked and here's what he said. Now, he hadn't said anything,

00:34:57.310 --> 00:35:03.710
but what they knew was that the FBI computer system and the CIA computer system were not

00:35:03.710 --> 00:35:09.550
integrated. So FBI personnel could not read CIA cables and CIA personnel could not read FBI

00:35:09.550 --> 00:35:14.350
cables. So they took everything that the FBI had reported on Abuzabeta, what he had said

00:35:14.350 --> 00:35:19.870
from the FBI interrogations. They put it into a CIA cable and said, here's what he said after

00:35:19.870 --> 00:35:24.830
we waterboarded him one time and they sent it in and people were like, oh my god, it actually works.

00:35:24.830 --> 00:35:30.190
This waterboarding works. We did it one time and he gave us this wealth of intelligence.

00:35:30.190 --> 00:35:35.150
Well, none of that was true. The information was stolen from the FBI. He had given it

00:35:35.150 --> 00:35:42.910
over the previous three months to Ali Sufan and they had completely failed in the waterboarding

00:35:42.910 --> 00:35:48.510
experiment. Besides the fact that waterboarding was supposed to be the last and harshest technique

00:35:48.510 --> 00:35:52.830
where they started with waterboarding. I'm not even going to ask if the spore ever held accountable

00:35:52.830 --> 00:35:58.750
because I know the answer to that question, but just lying like that. They're living happily

00:35:58.750 --> 00:36:03.550
ever after in their multi-million dollar beach houses in Florida.

00:36:03.550 --> 00:36:07.070
So when did you leave the CIA and what did you do after that?

00:36:07.070 --> 00:36:15.310
I left the CIA in March of 2004 and I went to Deloitte and Touche, which is one of the big four

00:36:15.310 --> 00:36:20.590
professional services firms. They had set up a competitive intelligence practice to spy on

00:36:20.590 --> 00:36:26.750
their competitors, try to figure out their pricing models, how discounts were determined,

00:36:27.310 --> 00:36:33.630
all very, very boring stuff, but amazing money. Amazing money. And I was tired of being shot at

00:36:33.630 --> 00:36:39.950
and people trying to kill me and plant bombs under my car and I was tired of it. Plus the fact that

00:36:39.950 --> 00:36:46.590
I had two kids that were now living with my ex-wife in Ohio. I wanted to get married again.

00:36:46.590 --> 00:36:51.630
I wanted to have more kids. And so I was assigned at the United Nations my last tour and I had

00:36:51.630 --> 00:36:57.230
to go to headquarters for a meeting and somebody had taped a flyer up on the wall in the elevator

00:36:57.230 --> 00:37:03.150
lobby for a class that was being offered and the class was entitled Raising Your Children in a War

00:37:03.150 --> 00:37:08.350
Zone. And I decided right there as I'm standing there that I was going to quit. And so I applied

00:37:08.350 --> 00:37:14.190
for this job at Deloitte. They gobbled me up after two weeks of interviews and I resigned.

00:37:14.190 --> 00:37:17.550
And at some point you work for the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, right?

00:37:17.550 --> 00:37:25.710
Yeah. That came after my whistleblowing. I had a good career at Deloitte. I was there for four years

00:37:25.710 --> 00:37:30.510
and then one day Brian Ross called me from ABC News and he said that he had a source who said

00:37:30.510 --> 00:37:36.350
that I had tortured Abu Zubedah. I said that was absolutely untrue, that I was the only person

00:37:36.350 --> 00:37:40.830
who was kind to Abu Zubedah. And he said, and this was an old reporter's trick that I didn't

00:37:40.830 --> 00:37:44.270
know because I had never spoken to a reporter before, but he said, well, you're welcome

00:37:44.270 --> 00:37:48.350
to come on the show and defend yourself. I said, I'll think about it. Well, the more I thought

00:37:48.350 --> 00:37:52.350
about it, the more I realized that Brian Ross's source was at the White House and they were going

00:37:52.350 --> 00:37:56.830
to try to pin this on me because word was leaking out that there was this torture program.

00:37:56.830 --> 00:38:02.430
ICRC had written about it. Human Rights Watch had written about it. Amnesty International.

00:38:03.070 --> 00:38:09.950
So I checked with my partner at Deloitte and he said, go ahead and go on the show.

00:38:10.030 --> 00:38:14.830
Don't mention Deloitte and don't mention any Deloitte clients. That was easy.

00:38:14.830 --> 00:38:19.870
So I went on the show and I said three things. I said that the CIA was torturing its prisoners,

00:38:19.870 --> 00:38:24.830
that torture was official U.S. government policy. It was not the result of a rogue

00:38:24.830 --> 00:38:28.510
and that the policy had been personally approved by the president himself.

00:38:28.510 --> 00:38:32.190
And you also said you felt like the policy shouldn't be secret because you think we should

00:38:32.190 --> 00:38:37.630
debate as a society. That is exactly right. That is exactly right. First of all,

00:38:37.630 --> 00:38:43.710
it's illegal in this country to classify a crime. If something is a criminal act by nature,

00:38:43.710 --> 00:38:51.310
it can't be classified. Right? In my view, torture was clearly illegal. And so to classify

00:38:51.310 --> 00:38:56.990
the program itself was illegal. It was my position that the American people owned the

00:38:56.990 --> 00:39:01.870
information, that the American people had a right to know what their government was doing

00:39:01.870 --> 00:39:06.270
in their name. And so I felt comfortable coming out with the information.

00:39:06.270 --> 00:39:12.670
And how does the CIA react to you doing this? The CIA, within 24 hours, filed what's called

00:39:12.670 --> 00:39:19.070
a crimes report against me with the FBI. They said that I had leaked classified information.

00:39:19.070 --> 00:39:24.510
The FBI investigated me for a full year from December of 2007 until December of 2008

00:39:24.510 --> 00:39:28.110
and then determined that I had not committed a crime. They closed the case.

00:39:28.110 --> 00:39:34.030
Three weeks later, Barack Obama became president and John Brennan, an old nemesis of mine,

00:39:34.030 --> 00:39:39.550
became the deputy national security advisor for counterterrorism. I did not know that Brennan

00:39:39.550 --> 00:39:45.230
then asked the Justice Department to secretly reopen the case against me. And so for three years,

00:39:45.230 --> 00:39:52.270
they investigated me. They tapped my phones. They intercepted my emails. They put surveillance

00:39:52.270 --> 00:39:58.510
teams on me periodically to follow me. And then finally in January of 2012, I was arrested.

00:39:59.150 --> 00:40:03.310
I was looking at the government accountability projects page on you. And they mentioned

00:40:03.390 --> 00:40:08.350
that as you kept talking to the media, the CIA kept filing more crimes reports that

00:40:08.350 --> 00:40:13.550
after the initial one, they filed at least six more. So they filed at least seven crimes reports

00:40:13.550 --> 00:40:19.070
on you. Funny. It's not funny. I mean, obviously, it's horrible that they did this to you.

00:40:19.070 --> 00:40:22.910
When did you first realize the FBI was investigating you? Because you've told

00:40:22.910 --> 00:40:28.350
this story several times. And every time I hear it, I am aghast anew and offended on your

00:40:28.350 --> 00:40:34.190
account again. And even though I know it's coming, it's just it's like it's an awful story.

00:40:34.190 --> 00:40:41.070
It's a horrible story. It is. I get this knot in my stomach even all these years later,

00:40:41.070 --> 00:40:46.190
almost a decade later. Well, after Deloitte, I went to the Senate Foreign Relations Committee.

00:40:46.190 --> 00:40:51.550
John Kerry hired me to be the senior investigator there. And I just couldn't seem to get my

00:40:51.550 --> 00:40:55.550
security clearance processed. Everybody else is getting their security clearances. And

00:40:55.550 --> 00:41:00.910
they kept telling me, oh, yours is delayed, yours is delayed. Okay, that should have been a major

00:41:00.910 --> 00:41:06.590
red flag for me. One of the things that I loved about that job was I got to have lunch with foreign

00:41:06.590 --> 00:41:11.310
diplomats all the time, about once a week, twice a week, maybe. And, you know, you talk

00:41:11.310 --> 00:41:16.190
about current events, and they go back and they write a cable, and then you go back and you

00:41:16.190 --> 00:41:21.790
write a memo, you send it around. So I got an invitation from a Japanese diplomat to have

00:41:21.790 --> 00:41:26.590
lunch and we got together on Capitol Hill and talked. I remember the conversation.

00:41:26.590 --> 00:41:32.270
We talked about Israeli elections, Turkish elections, and the Arab-Israeli peace process.

00:41:32.270 --> 00:41:37.070
And at the end of the conversation, he says to me, so what's next for you? And I said, well,

00:41:37.070 --> 00:41:42.270
I think I'm going to resign soon. I promised Senator Kerry that I'd give him two years and

00:41:42.270 --> 00:41:47.550
it's been two and a half. And by then I had five children. And I said, I got to make some money

00:41:47.550 --> 00:41:53.630
and put my kids through college. So he says, no, no, don't do that. If you give me information,

00:41:53.630 --> 00:41:58.990
I can give you money. And I said, shame on you. What's the matter with you? Do you have any idea

00:41:58.990 --> 00:42:06.910
how many times I've made that pitch? I said, I have to report this. I got up and I went directly

00:42:06.910 --> 00:42:11.310
to the office of the Senate Security Officer. And I said, I was just pitched by a foreign

00:42:11.310 --> 00:42:16.430
intelligence officer. He told me to write it up as a memo. He sent it to the FBI. And

00:42:16.430 --> 00:42:20.830
the next day, two FBI agents came over to the Senate to interview me. And they said, here's

00:42:20.830 --> 00:42:26.110
what we want you to do. We want you to call them back and invite him to lunch and try to get him

00:42:26.110 --> 00:42:31.710
to tell you exactly what information he wants and how much money he's willing to pay for it.

00:42:31.710 --> 00:42:36.430
We'll be at the next table. I said, okay, well, they called me the day of the lunch to say

00:42:36.430 --> 00:42:40.110
something had come up. They couldn't be there, but just to write them another memo. So I

00:42:40.110 --> 00:42:44.910
took the guy to lunch. I got the information. I wrote a memo. They asked me to do it a

00:42:44.910 --> 00:42:51.230
second time and a third time and a fourth time, which I did. And every time I wrote them a memo.

00:42:51.230 --> 00:42:57.630
A year later, fast forward a year, I've just been arrested, charged with five felonies,

00:42:57.630 --> 00:43:02.350
including three counts of espionage for speaking to ABC News and the New York Times.

00:43:02.910 --> 00:43:09.230
And we get 15,000 pages of discovery. And in discovery, we find several very interesting

00:43:09.230 --> 00:43:15.950
things. First, there never was any Japanese diplomat. He was an FBI agent undercover,

00:43:15.950 --> 00:43:23.310
trying to get me to commit real espionage, money for information. But I kept reporting the contact

00:43:23.310 --> 00:43:30.830
back to the FBI. We finally found a memo in discovery saying, we should end this operation.

00:43:30.830 --> 00:43:36.110
He's clearly not going to take the bait. They said he keeps reporting the contact back to us.

00:43:36.750 --> 00:43:42.430
So they ended the operation. And then the Japanese diplomat said he got transferred to Cairo. He shook

00:43:42.430 --> 00:43:47.310
my hand. I never saw him again. Well, he's an FBI agent. The other memos that we found in

00:43:47.310 --> 00:43:53.070
discovery were a memo from John Brennan to the Justice Department saying, charge him with

00:43:53.070 --> 00:43:57.950
espionage. And then Eric Holder wrote back and said, my people don't think he committed

00:43:57.950 --> 00:44:02.990
espionage. And John Brennan wrote charge him anyway and make him defend himself. And they

00:44:02.990 --> 00:44:07.230
did. And then when I went bankrupt, they dropped the charges. That's what they do.

00:44:07.230 --> 00:44:14.430
There's also during this same time, a parallel investigation that sometimes some of the media

00:44:14.430 --> 00:44:20.190
reports get sort of interwoven with yours. And I think it's worth bringing up that

00:44:20.190 --> 00:44:24.910
the ACLU starts its John Adams project to represent individuals held at Guantanamo Bay

00:44:24.910 --> 00:44:29.710
Prison. Obviously, it's a reference to John Adams, who defended one of the British

00:44:29.790 --> 00:44:36.350
redcoats who fired on the crowds. Looking at the defense, he gave the soldier in light of sort of

00:44:36.350 --> 00:44:41.230
recent events. It's interesting because he draws on the multiracial nature of the crowd.

00:44:41.230 --> 00:44:46.750
Anyone would have been afraid of a motley crew of black men and sailors, which is an interesting

00:44:46.750 --> 00:44:51.630
thing to think about now. But I'll table that for a minute. So in order to raise the issue

00:44:51.630 --> 00:44:55.470
of their client's torture before these military commissions, they have to get their clients

00:44:55.470 --> 00:45:00.590
to identify the torture. They use a photo lineup, a private investigator, various techniques to get

00:45:00.590 --> 00:45:06.110
their identities. And then the attorneys file, and I really want to stress, a classified motion

00:45:06.110 --> 00:45:12.190
under SEAL for discovery that lists the name of the CIA torturers or alleged torturers. And the

00:45:12.190 --> 00:45:19.550
CIA is so outraged they file a crimes report. And Eric Holder, Obama's attorney general,

00:45:19.550 --> 00:45:26.430
tasked Patrick Fitzgerald, who is, of course, of the Valerie Plame fame, to criminally investigate

00:45:26.430 --> 00:45:32.510
at the request of the CIA, the ACLU and these Guantanamo Bay lawyers. Only time in the ACLU's

00:45:32.510 --> 00:45:37.150
history they've ever been a subject of a criminal investigation. How does this sort of factor

00:45:37.150 --> 00:45:42.830
into what happens to you? This played a major role in what ended up happening to me.

00:45:42.830 --> 00:45:50.990
So as you said, the Guantanamo defense lawyers presented the judge in the Guantanamo hearings

00:45:50.990 --> 00:45:58.270
with a list of names. The judge recognized that the names were classified because they were

00:45:58.270 --> 00:46:04.190
either active duty or recently active duty CIA officers alleged to be involved in the torture

00:46:04.190 --> 00:46:11.870
program. So he asked the FBI to investigate. The FBI asked the Guantanamo defense attorneys

00:46:11.870 --> 00:46:17.390
where they got the names. They got the names from an investigator for Human Rights Watch by the

00:46:17.390 --> 00:46:23.230
name of John Sifton. They went to Sifton and asked where he got the names. Instead of saying no

00:46:23.230 --> 00:46:30.510
comment, Sifton said, I got the names from Matthew Cole. Matthew Cole at the time was a

00:46:30.510 --> 00:46:37.710
journalist with ABC News. And Matthew Cole had sent me a list of a dozen names and said,

00:46:37.710 --> 00:46:43.230
hey, I'm writing a book. Can you help me get an interview with any of these dozen people? I said,

00:46:43.230 --> 00:46:47.630
I don't know who these people are. He sent me a second list of a dozen names. And again, I said,

00:46:47.630 --> 00:46:52.030
I don't know any of these people. You obviously know this issue far better than I do. I can't

00:46:52.030 --> 00:46:57.070
help you. And he said, what about the guy that you reference on page 100 and whatever

00:46:57.070 --> 00:47:02.990
of your book? I think his name is, I'll say John. And I said, and this is my crime,

00:47:02.990 --> 00:47:07.710
oh, you're talking about John Doe. I don't know whatever happened to him. He's probably

00:47:07.710 --> 00:47:12.910
retired and living in Virginia somewhere. I had confirmed the last name of one of the people.

00:47:12.910 --> 00:47:18.590
So they went, the FBI went to Cole and said, where did you get the name? Cole ratted me out

00:47:18.590 --> 00:47:25.310
to the FBI. And then later testified against me in the grand jury. As it turned out, Cole was

00:47:25.310 --> 00:47:33.310
on the Guantanamo defense attorney's payroll. He was moonlighting from ABC and never told ABC

00:47:33.310 --> 00:47:39.550
that he was doing this. He was presenting himself as a journalist working on a story. He was not.

00:47:39.550 --> 00:47:47.710
Brian Ross told me later that that day, the day of my arrest, Cole went to Brian and said,

00:47:47.710 --> 00:47:52.590
I think I really screwed up. And he told Brian what he had done. Brian called the

00:47:52.590 --> 00:47:58.350
vice president for news. The vice president for news called security and they escorted him out of

00:47:58.350 --> 00:48:03.310
the building and took his badge. And he was fired. But that didn't stop him from ratting me out.

00:48:03.310 --> 00:48:08.910
Later on, he went to the intercept where he ratted out reality winner and Terry Albury and

00:48:08.910 --> 00:48:15.950
God knows who else. But yeah, this was people who I had heretofore respected and admired

00:48:15.950 --> 00:48:19.630
people like John Sifton. John Sifton's legendary in the human rights community.

00:48:19.630 --> 00:48:25.230
But he very willingly and readily worked with the FBI to prosecute me.

00:48:25.230 --> 00:48:29.070
So what are the charges they bring against you? Could you go into them a little bit?

00:48:29.070 --> 00:48:35.870
Yeah. They brought three charges of espionage, two for speaking with the New York Times and

00:48:35.870 --> 00:48:42.670
one for the ABC News interview. One count of violating the Intelligence Identities Protection

00:48:42.670 --> 00:48:48.830
Act of 1981 for confirming the last name, which was never made public, by the way.

00:48:48.830 --> 00:48:54.670
No one ever got the last name. And one count of making a false statement. And we were never

00:48:54.670 --> 00:48:58.590
exactly clear as to what the false statement was supposed to have been.

00:48:58.590 --> 00:49:04.190
It was with the classification of you for your book. Yeah. I read through the indictment.

00:49:04.190 --> 00:49:10.510
I always read through the indictment before doing these interviews. It was a confusing claim.

00:49:10.510 --> 00:49:16.110
So what ends up happening in your... Yeah. On the day of my arrest, we have a proffer

00:49:16.110 --> 00:49:21.070
meeting and there was this woman there from the Justice Department and she kept saying,

00:49:21.070 --> 00:49:28.430
45 years, Mr. Kiriakou, 45 years. Take a plea and you might live to meet your grandchildren.

00:49:28.430 --> 00:49:33.230
Well, I've gone up against some pretty tough people, so I'm not going to let this woman

00:49:33.230 --> 00:49:38.430
intimidate me. Plus I'm represented by literally the greatest legal minds in Washington,

00:49:38.430 --> 00:49:42.830
right? Seriously. Legends in the Washington legal community.

00:49:42.830 --> 00:49:49.470
So Patrick Fitzgerald offered 10 years. Take a plea to an espionage charge and do 10 years.

00:49:49.470 --> 00:49:54.990
I said, I didn't commit espionage. I mean, it's literally as simple as that. I didn't commit

00:49:54.990 --> 00:50:00.510
espionage. That was a Monday. Two days later, Wednesday, they came down to eight years.

00:50:00.510 --> 00:50:06.190
And then on Friday, they came down to five years. And my lead attorney, Plato Kacheris,

00:50:06.190 --> 00:50:12.270
again, a legendary figure who's represented Monica Lewinsky and Aldrich Ames and former

00:50:12.270 --> 00:50:16.430
attorney general John Mitchell through Watergate and going back to the fifties.

00:50:17.070 --> 00:50:23.710
He said to me, I have been an attorney for 52 years and I have never seen them come down in

00:50:23.710 --> 00:50:28.350
time like this. And I said, well, what does that mean? And he said, it means they have a

00:50:28.350 --> 00:50:33.550
shit case and they know it's shit and we're going to trial, he says. That made me feel

00:50:33.550 --> 00:50:39.230
much better. Well, they were stuck at five years for the next 10 months. They didn't

00:50:39.230 --> 00:50:44.270
budge. In the meantime, four months after that proffer meeting, Fitzgerald resigns,

00:50:45.310 --> 00:50:51.470
which we also took to be very good news for us. And then finally, we have a meeting in

00:50:51.470 --> 00:50:57.790
October of 2012 and they said, five years, take it or leave it. If you leave it,

00:50:57.790 --> 00:51:03.870
we're going for the full 45 years. So I said, I didn't do anything wrong.

00:51:03.870 --> 00:51:09.150
And my attorneys want to go to trial. And if we go to trial, I'm going to testify on

00:51:09.150 --> 00:51:15.230
my own behalf. And I am going to talk about some of the hideous crimes against humanity

00:51:15.230 --> 00:51:21.470
and war crimes that I witnessed in 15 years at the CIA. Then they came back and they said,

00:51:22.110 --> 00:51:27.870
three years, right? We said, we'll take one. We'll do one year. And then they said,

00:51:27.870 --> 00:51:33.550
best and final offer, two and a half years you do two. So I had 24 hours to think about it.

00:51:33.550 --> 00:51:38.830
My wife and I stayed up all night long. They were going to drop the espionage charges,

00:51:38.830 --> 00:51:42.990
drop the false statements charge because they were all weak. I mean, the only thing that I had

00:51:42.990 --> 00:51:48.030
actually done is I did confirm the last name of that former colleague. I hadn't committed

00:51:48.030 --> 00:51:53.230
espionage. I hadn't made any false statement. So I was only the second American ever charged

00:51:53.230 --> 00:51:57.310
with violating the Intelligence Identities Protection Act. The only other person was a

00:51:57.310 --> 00:52:04.910
woman in the 1980s who she was a CIA secretary sleeping with a member of the Ghanaian Intelligence

00:52:04.910 --> 00:52:10.430
Service. And she gave to him all of the names of the CIA's assets, which the Ghanaians then

00:52:10.430 --> 00:52:14.670
rounded up and executed. So there's a big difference between her case and my case. And

00:52:14.670 --> 00:52:21.230
she got six months. So I called my attorneys. I didn't call them. I emailed my attorneys

00:52:21.230 --> 00:52:25.470
at six in the morning. I said, my wife and I were up all night and I decided I didn't

00:52:25.470 --> 00:52:30.350
do anything wrong and I'm going to trial. Plato Kacheris emails me back and he says,

00:52:30.350 --> 00:52:36.430
you stupid son of a bitch take the deal. And then, which was like the normal way that Plato spoke.

00:52:36.430 --> 00:52:41.230
So then one of the other attorneys says, put on a pot of coffee. We're on our way to your house.

00:52:41.230 --> 00:52:46.270
Two of the other leads came over. One was the, he's the director of the white collar crime

00:52:46.270 --> 00:52:52.350
practice at Aiken Gump and Strauss, arguably one of the most important law firms in the world.

00:52:52.430 --> 00:52:59.710
And the other was Bob Trout of Trout Kacheris. So they came to the house and they were angry with me.

00:53:00.270 --> 00:53:06.510
And Mark McDougal from Aiken Gump said something that it's just stuck in my mind all these years.

00:53:06.510 --> 00:53:10.270
He got right in my face and he said, you know what your problem is? Your problem is you

00:53:10.270 --> 00:53:15.710
think about, you think this is about justice and it's not about justice. It's about mitigating

00:53:15.710 --> 00:53:21.070
damage. Take the deal. And then the other attorney said, John, if you were my brother,

00:53:21.070 --> 00:53:27.950
I would beg you to take this deal. He said, this can be a blip in your life or it can be the defining

00:53:27.950 --> 00:53:35.230
event of your life. Make it the blip. And so I took the deal. I also knew that ProPublica had

00:53:35.230 --> 00:53:41.550
just come out with a study saying that the federal government wins 98.2% of its cases.

00:53:41.550 --> 00:53:46.750
They do charge stacking. They do venue shopping. They drive you to bankruptcy.

00:53:46.750 --> 00:53:55.390
They spent $6 million prosecuting me, $6 million. I still owe my lawyers $880,000 that they'll

00:53:55.390 --> 00:54:01.870
never see. I'll never have that kind of money. So I knew that my chances of winning were fewer

00:54:01.870 --> 00:54:09.150
than two in a hundred. And the other thing was I said to the lawyers, if we go to trial and

00:54:09.150 --> 00:54:15.710
we lose, realistically, what am I looking at? And they said 12 to 18 years. Take the deal.

00:54:15.790 --> 00:54:17.150
So I took the deal.

00:54:17.150 --> 00:54:18.910
What was your time in prison like?

00:54:18.910 --> 00:54:22.350
Well, I wrote a book about it actually called Doing Time Like a Spy,

00:54:22.350 --> 00:54:27.550
How the CIA Taught Me to Survive and Thrive in Prison. It was more difficult than I had expected

00:54:27.550 --> 00:54:32.990
because at sentencing, my attorneys asked the judge to send me to a minimum security work camp

00:54:32.990 --> 00:54:37.630
and she agreed and the prosecution agreed. And then when I got to the minimum security

00:54:37.630 --> 00:54:42.750
work camp, they kind of laughed at me and sent me across the street to the actual prison

00:54:42.750 --> 00:54:49.230
with the concertina wire and double fences and guard towers with the guys with sniper rifles.

00:54:49.230 --> 00:54:55.390
So it took me five days to get access to a phone and I called the lawyers and I said,

00:54:55.390 --> 00:55:00.990
hey, they put me in the actual prison with the mafia dons and the pedophiles and the drug

00:55:00.990 --> 00:55:06.990
kingpins. What do I do? And the lawyer said, oh my God, he said, well, we could file a motion,

00:55:06.990 --> 00:55:10.590
but it'll be two years before we get a hearing on it and you'll be home by then.

00:55:11.470 --> 00:55:17.550
I'm sorry, buddy. You're just going to have to tough it out. And so I decided that day that I was

00:55:17.550 --> 00:55:23.470
trained for this. I was trained for a lot worse than this and that I was going to use my CIA

00:55:23.470 --> 00:55:30.350
skills to remain safe and remain at the top of the social heap. And so that's what I did.

00:55:30.350 --> 00:55:35.870
You've also spoken before about your cellmate, Jim, and the sort of the treatment he endured.

00:55:35.870 --> 00:55:40.190
Could you talk a little bit about that because you've mentioned a couple of times that

00:55:40.270 --> 00:55:43.710
throughout your time in prison, you came to that torture wasn't just something that the U.S.

00:55:43.710 --> 00:55:48.670
was doing. It was happening in America's prisons. This poor son of a gun, he was,

00:55:50.030 --> 00:55:57.790
where do you even start? He was homeless. He had done some time on a federal sexual

00:55:57.790 --> 00:56:04.030
procurement charge where he was a carny. He worked at a traveling carnival. This is exactly

00:56:04.030 --> 00:56:09.550
why I don't allow my children to go to carnivals or county fairs. They're all dangerous,

00:56:10.030 --> 00:56:16.910
people, and most of them are pedophiles. So he was a carny and his boss saw some girl that he

00:56:16.910 --> 00:56:21.950
wanted to sleep with and he told Jim, hey, you hook me up with that girl over there. So Jim went

00:56:21.950 --> 00:56:26.990
over and got this girl and gave her some money and the boss ended up having sex with her. Well,

00:56:26.990 --> 00:56:33.630
here she was 16 years old. So the boss was charged with a federal child sex crime and Jim

00:56:33.630 --> 00:56:40.430
was charged with procurement for lack of a better term. So he did, I think he did like four years

00:56:40.990 --> 00:56:48.590
and they released him, but he's homeless. He's got a lifelong drug problem and he ended up living

00:56:48.590 --> 00:56:57.790
in a cardboard box under a bridge in Pittsburgh. And he told me that he had purposely violated

00:56:57.790 --> 00:57:02.510
his probation because winter was coming and he just didn't want to live in the box through

00:57:02.510 --> 00:57:06.670
winter. That it was better to be in prison where he could at least have a bed and some

00:57:06.670 --> 00:57:12.590
heating and medical care and food. And that's what he did. Well, it came time to get released

00:57:12.590 --> 00:57:18.350
because you violate probation, you get like 90 days, right? Well, it's still cold out. So

00:57:18.350 --> 00:57:25.070
then he attacks a guard and we're like, what are you thinking, man? What's the matter with

00:57:25.070 --> 00:57:30.030
you? And he said, no, if I attack the guard, I get another 60 days in solitary. That way,

00:57:30.030 --> 00:57:36.190
when I get out, it's going to be April and it'll be warm. And that's what we've come to in this country.

00:57:36.190 --> 00:57:41.150
Solitary confinement, the United Nations has declared the US use of solitary confinement

00:57:41.150 --> 00:57:46.190
as a form of torture and they recommend no more than 15 days that a person be held in

00:57:46.190 --> 00:57:51.070
solitary for no more than 15 consecutive days. You get one shower a week, you get one phone

00:57:51.070 --> 00:57:58.350
call a month and you literally cannot leave your six by 10 foot cube or whatever it is

00:57:58.350 --> 00:58:03.310
for the duration of the solitary confinement or your muscles waste away. And because the prisons

00:58:03.310 --> 00:58:08.990
are so overcrowded, you're not really solitary. There's a guy sleeping on the floor. Sometimes

00:58:08.990 --> 00:58:13.310
you have to have a fist fight over who gets the bed. People get killed that way. And the

00:58:13.310 --> 00:58:17.790
guards don't care. They just let you scrap it out. The stronger guy wins the weaker guy

00:58:17.790 --> 00:58:23.950
sleeps on the floor. And this happens in every prison all across America. And in the meantime,

00:58:23.950 --> 00:58:30.270
that 15-day guideline is laughed at. We have people in solitary confinement in the United States

00:58:30.270 --> 00:58:37.070
for more than 40 years. There was a guy in Louisiana, 44 years in solitary confinement

00:58:37.070 --> 00:58:42.350
without any human contact. Well, those kinds of people can't function in society. You've

00:58:43.550 --> 00:58:48.110
stripped them of their humanity. You've taken their ability to live in society away.

00:58:48.110 --> 00:58:51.390
And I used to see it with my own eyes when I was in prison.

00:58:51.390 --> 00:58:54.990
While you're in prison, you send an open letter to Edward Snowden.

00:58:54.990 --> 00:58:59.950
What did you say in this letter? Yeah, I was in prison six months when Ed Snowden went public

00:58:59.950 --> 00:59:06.030
with his revelations. And I felt like I knew what he was going through. I knew why he did what he did.

00:59:06.030 --> 00:59:10.750
He did it for the public good. And I wanted to show my support, but I wanted to show support

00:59:10.750 --> 00:59:15.230
in a tangible way. I didn't want to just say, Hey, Ed, you know, we're with you, man.

00:59:15.230 --> 00:59:19.630
I wanted to help. So I wrote two letters as a matter of fact. One was an open letter

00:59:19.630 --> 00:59:24.510
and one was a private letter. And I told him not to make the mistakes that I made,

00:59:24.510 --> 00:59:30.190
get the best lawyers money can buy, get my lawyers, I recommended to him, which he ended up doing.

00:59:30.750 --> 00:59:37.630
I said, don't talk to anybody. You have to just assume that everybody is spying for the FBI.

00:59:38.190 --> 00:59:43.150
And that happened to me in prison. We can get to that if you want. And I said,

00:59:43.150 --> 00:59:48.030
cultivate your support on Capitol Hill. More people support you than you realize.

00:59:48.030 --> 00:59:52.990
They're just afraid to say something. And then privately, I sort of laid things out point by

00:59:52.990 --> 00:59:59.790
point by point. And I sent them to a CIA friend of mine. And he got the private letter to Snowden.

00:59:59.790 --> 01:00:06.510
And then Ed's dad came to visit me in prison. And as did this CIA friend of mine,

01:00:06.510 --> 01:00:10.910
and we were able to pass messages back and forth. We made sure not to put anything

01:00:10.910 --> 01:00:16.670
in writing beyond my very first letter. But, you know, I knew exactly what the guy was

01:00:16.670 --> 01:00:21.230
going through. And I wanted to help. That's incredible. And you've been very outspoken

01:00:21.230 --> 01:00:26.110
for other whistleblowers as well. I know you and I were both at Daniel Hale's sentencing.

01:00:26.110 --> 01:00:31.310
Yes. The drone whistleblower. I was very, very pleased to see in the Washington Post this morning

01:00:31.310 --> 01:00:37.070
that- Ilan Omar. Ilan Omar is asking President Biden to pardon Daniel. Did the Post publish

01:00:37.070 --> 01:00:41.710
that already? Yes. Yes. I knew in advance they were going to do that. I didn't know it's

01:00:41.710 --> 01:00:49.870
coming out. Yeah. We can talk about that sometime off air. So Gina Haspel is involved in this torture.

01:00:49.870 --> 01:00:57.310
And Trump- Heavily. Heavily. And Trump nominates her to be the CIA director. During this time,

01:00:57.310 --> 01:01:00.830
a number of groups like defending rights in the Senate and others are meeting with members of

01:01:00.830 --> 01:01:05.070
Congress, asking them not to confirm her nomination. It actually seems like there's

01:01:05.070 --> 01:01:08.670
going to be a number of Republicans, including some surprising ones who are going to vote

01:01:08.670 --> 01:01:14.350
against her. But these sort of so-called moderate Democrats like Clara McCauskel all come public,

01:01:14.350 --> 01:01:19.710
say we're going to vote for Gina Haspel. So that scares off the Republicans. And we end up with

01:01:19.710 --> 01:01:24.590
a war criminal of the CIA director, though probably not the first time. But you wrote

01:01:24.590 --> 01:01:29.950
something really powerful in the Washington Post about Gina Haspel and her nomination.

01:01:29.950 --> 01:01:35.230
You wrote, the message this sends to the CIA workforce is simple. Engage in war crimes

01:01:35.310 --> 01:01:41.070
and crimes in humanity, and you'll get promoted. You know, the CIA tried to redact that sentence.

01:01:41.070 --> 01:01:44.830
Really? I had to fight them on it. Because of the pre-publication review?

01:01:44.830 --> 01:01:49.230
Uh-huh. I sent it in for pre-publication review. They hated the whole op-ed, of course.

01:01:49.230 --> 01:01:54.190
I'm sure they did. I mean, I've read it. I liked it, but I can imagine

01:01:55.390 --> 01:02:00.590
what the people who are filing crime reports and you for going on ABC News and the ACLU lawyers

01:02:00.590 --> 01:02:05.630
for like doing basic defense work, I could imagine what they saw when they read that.

01:02:05.630 --> 01:02:11.070
Yeah, they said that. I said, listen, you're only allowed to redact classified information.

01:02:11.070 --> 01:02:14.990
And John Kiriak's opinion that Gina Haspel is a war criminal is not classified.

01:02:15.710 --> 01:02:19.310
If they're saying that's classified, that's almost an acknowledgement that it's true.

01:02:19.310 --> 01:02:20.910
It's an admission. Yeah, it's an admission.

01:02:20.910 --> 01:02:23.070
That Gina Haspel is a war, wow.

01:02:23.070 --> 01:02:28.350
Yeah, I worked for Gina Haspel when she was the number three in the counterterrorism center.

01:02:28.830 --> 01:02:36.030
She was a protégé of Jose Rodriguez, who is one of the most notorious operations officers

01:02:36.030 --> 01:02:42.590
to serve at the CIA in its modern history. I believed that Jose was a war criminal.

01:02:42.590 --> 01:02:47.710
In addition to being a sexual harasser and constantly in trouble because he can't keep

01:02:47.710 --> 01:02:51.390
his hands to himself, and what do they do? They just keep promoting him and promoting

01:02:51.390 --> 01:02:56.190
him and promoting him. And so when he becomes the head of counterterrorism,

01:02:56.190 --> 01:03:03.470
he makes Gina chief of staff number three in the office. Then he becomes the deputy director for

01:03:03.470 --> 01:03:11.790
operations. And Gina becomes, well, Gina became the chief of very, very large and important

01:03:11.790 --> 01:03:18.110
overseas station. And then it looks like she's going to retire. She becomes the,

01:03:18.110 --> 01:03:23.310
what was she? The deputy director, I guess. And then Trump makes her director.

01:03:23.550 --> 01:03:27.790
The morning that my op-ed ran in the Washington Post, I got a call from Rand Paul,

01:03:27.790 --> 01:03:33.390
and he says, can you come to my office and strategize with us about how we can block this

01:03:33.390 --> 01:03:39.150
woman? I said, I'll be there in 20 minutes. So I rushed to Capitol Hill. He's got this whole

01:03:39.150 --> 01:03:44.750
team of people. They're counting votes, who's yes, who's no, who's maybe, who can we lobby.

01:03:44.750 --> 01:03:48.670
And then he ends up voting yes. It was shocking to me. But like you said,

01:03:48.670 --> 01:03:54.430
it was the Democrats that did that. I, Rand Paul is one of the Republicans whose office I met with,

01:03:54.430 --> 01:04:00.990
not the surprising one. The surprising one was, well, I won't say. And they were so gung-ho

01:04:00.990 --> 01:04:05.390
about it. And it was really very disappointing. Disappointing isn't even the right word for it.

01:04:05.390 --> 01:04:08.830
I mean, you're giving your authority, you're giving your approval of a war criminal. That's

01:04:08.830 --> 01:04:12.510
beyond disappointment. You know, that's not like you don't have your favorite ice cream at

01:04:12.510 --> 01:04:20.670
the store. That's well beyond that. So this is a podcast about whistleblowers. But I would be remiss

01:04:20.670 --> 01:04:24.350
if I, and I know we're getting short on time and you've already gone over what I've asked you to do,

01:04:24.350 --> 01:04:29.470
so I'm cognizant of that. But I would be remiss if I do not ask you about your meeting with

01:04:29.470 --> 01:04:34.510
that distinguished member of the bar, Rudy Giuliani. And what he and his associate told

01:04:34.510 --> 01:04:39.710
you about a pardon. Very distinguished jurist, Rudy Giuliani, right?

01:04:40.670 --> 01:04:44.030
Rudy Giuliani. I wanted to punch him in the nose, but that's a whole different issue.

01:04:44.030 --> 01:04:50.670
You're not the only one in this country. You know, Chip, my arrest and conviction

01:04:50.670 --> 01:04:56.350
had a way of freeing me politically. I used to be kind of your run-of-the-mill Democrat,

01:04:56.350 --> 01:05:03.630
even if I was on the left wing of the Democratic Party. I left the Democratic Party in 2012,

01:05:03.630 --> 01:05:06.830
and I sort of bounced between the Greens and the Libertarians.

01:05:06.830 --> 01:05:14.350
But the enemy of my enemy is my friend, right? And so I've reserved very harsh criticism for

01:05:14.350 --> 01:05:21.870
Barack Obama, Eric Holder, and John Brennan. And that sort of drew the attention of Fox News.

01:05:21.870 --> 01:05:32.990
And so I started going on Fox News in 2000, I guess it was 16 or 17, and struck up a friendship

01:05:32.990 --> 01:05:38.510
with Tucker Carlson. I've been on Tucker's show, I don't know, nine or 10 times,

01:05:38.510 --> 01:05:43.790
because they want me to beat up on Obama and Holder. And I'm happy to do it, right? Because they

01:05:43.790 --> 01:05:50.110
wronged me. Not to say I agree with these guys on anything else, but on these whistleblowing

01:05:50.110 --> 01:05:56.030
issues, they've always been supportive of me. Interestingly enough, MSNBC still refers to

01:05:56.030 --> 01:06:03.630
me as CIA leaker John Kiriakou. CNN started calling me CIA whistleblower John Kiriakou.

01:06:03.630 --> 01:06:08.270
The week that I went to prison, it took my attorneys, especially Jaisalyn Radak,

01:06:08.270 --> 01:06:13.870
a full year to get them to stop calling me a leaker and stop and start calling me a whistleblower.

01:06:13.870 --> 01:06:18.670
Fox, from the day of my arrest, called me a whistleblower, and I don't know why.

01:06:18.670 --> 01:06:24.670
So for that, I'm grateful. So I had been talking to Tucker about a pardon and about

01:06:24.670 --> 01:06:31.550
getting directly in front of Donald Trump and pleading my case. And Tucker was immensely helpful.

01:06:31.550 --> 01:06:35.790
One of my appearances on his show led to a face-to-face meeting with Jared Kushner,

01:06:35.790 --> 01:06:44.030
which didn't go anywhere. Trump tweeted my name and something that I had said on Tucker's show.

01:06:44.030 --> 01:06:49.870
I thought maybe that was going to help. Nothing helped. So finally, I say, look,

01:06:49.870 --> 01:06:56.270
I'd really love to meet with Giuliani. So I get in touch with Giuliani's Major Domo,

01:06:56.270 --> 01:07:01.390
who's a former partner at Hydric and Struggles. And he knew who I was. I said,

01:07:01.390 --> 01:07:06.510
look, I really need a pardon. A pardon gets me my federal pension back, and I really need it.

01:07:06.510 --> 01:07:11.950
And so he said, well, as it stands, we're going to be in Washington next week. Why don't we

01:07:11.950 --> 01:07:16.350
get together at the Trump Hotel? I said, great. Maybe we can meet for dinner or whatever.

01:07:16.350 --> 01:07:23.950
He said, no. He said, Giuliani really loves Scotch, meaning he drinks it like by the bottle,

01:07:23.950 --> 01:07:32.830
like I drink Diet Coke. And so it's better to meet before two o'clock, meaning he's usually

01:07:32.830 --> 01:07:41.230
too drunk by two and you can't do any business. So we met for lunch at the Trump Hotel in Washington.

01:07:41.230 --> 01:07:47.390
And we're sitting there. It's like four or five of us. And they're laughing and talking and people

01:07:47.390 --> 01:07:52.910
are coming up and shaking his hand and asking him for a picture and an autograph. And then finally,

01:07:52.910 --> 01:08:00.110
I said, so there's the issue of my pardon. And he says, I got to hit the head and he gets up

01:08:00.110 --> 01:08:05.230
and walks away. And I said, what the heck just happened? And his number two says, Rudy

01:08:05.230 --> 01:08:10.350
never talks money. You got to talk money with me. And then I'll talk with him. I said,

01:08:10.350 --> 01:08:15.870
all right, whatever. He said, so Rudy's going to want two million bucks. And I said, two million

01:08:15.870 --> 01:08:22.910
bucks. Are you crazy? I said, first of all, I don't have two million bucks. Secondly, why would I spend

01:08:22.910 --> 01:08:29.870
two million dollars to recover a $700,000 pension? That doesn't make any sense at all. And so

01:08:29.870 --> 01:08:34.910
Rudy came back and sat down and kept drinking. And I said, thank you for your time. And that

01:08:34.910 --> 01:08:41.150
was the end of it. So I went to a book party about a month later, and there was another whistleblower

01:08:41.150 --> 01:08:46.590
there. And he said, how are things going toward your pardon? I said, oh, listen to this, man. I

01:08:46.590 --> 01:08:50.670
said, I had drinks with Giuliani a couple of weeks ago. I wanted to sock the guy in the face.

01:08:50.670 --> 01:08:55.470
So I told him the story. And he said, well, that's illegal, what Giuliani did. I said,

01:08:55.470 --> 01:08:59.950
yeah, well, you know what? I've been in Washington 39 years. That's how Washington works.

01:09:00.030 --> 01:09:06.590
So my friend, unbeknownst to me, called the FBI and reported Giuliani to the FBI. And the FBI

01:09:06.590 --> 01:09:12.030
didn't care. They weren't interested. And he was so angry that they weren't interested that he called

01:09:12.030 --> 01:09:16.110
Mike Schmidt at the New York Times and told him the whole story. So Mike Schmidt calls me. And

01:09:16.110 --> 01:09:19.870
he said, hey, listen, a friend of yours called me and told me that you had this lunch with

01:09:19.870 --> 01:09:24.670
Giuliani. I said, oh, crap. And I just happened to be in my attorney's office

01:09:24.670 --> 01:09:28.110
when Mike Schmidt called me. He said, I'd really love it if you could go on the record.

01:09:28.110 --> 01:09:32.990
And I said, you know what? Talk to my attorney. So here's my attorney. So he talks to my attorney.

01:09:32.990 --> 01:09:37.150
And we decide, no, I have nothing to lose. I'm not going to get this pardon.

01:09:37.150 --> 01:09:41.310
So I'm going to go on the record. And I was one of very few people who went on.

01:09:41.310 --> 01:09:45.870
There were 100 people that were off the record, but I went on the record and it was on the front

01:09:45.870 --> 01:09:51.950
page of the New York Times. I went to work. I went to work and my co-host Brian Becker

01:09:51.950 --> 01:09:56.830
is like, can't you stay out of the papers front page of the New York Times? I said,

01:09:56.830 --> 01:10:01.390
I know, I know. What can I do? Sometimes I like to just throw a grenade into the middle of the room.

01:10:01.390 --> 01:10:05.710
I remember when that story came out and you're looking at it like, oh, wait, is that fat John

01:10:05.710 --> 01:10:12.270
Carriac? You know, I can't be John. It has to be someone else. And it's just like, oh my God.

01:10:13.470 --> 01:10:18.510
So I guess I have two final questions for you. And then I'm going to let you go because

01:10:18.510 --> 01:10:22.750
you've already given us more than we told you we would have you for. Do you have any regrets

01:10:22.750 --> 01:10:27.710
about any of this? Looking back on going forward with the information about torture,

01:10:27.710 --> 01:10:33.310
they put you in jail, they bankrupted you, they spotted you, they hounded you. Do you ever regret

01:10:33.310 --> 01:10:39.550
your choice to go forward? No. The easy answer is no. Never. I've never, ever doubted myself.

01:10:39.550 --> 01:10:44.510
I've never second guessed myself. And the only advice that I give other whistleblowers or

01:10:44.510 --> 01:10:48.990
potential whistleblowers, something that I would do differently is to hire my attorneys

01:10:48.990 --> 01:10:53.790
before blowing the whistle. That's the only mistake I made. That's very good advice.

01:10:53.790 --> 01:10:57.550
And when Jess Lynn was on our program, that was the advice she gave to.

01:10:57.550 --> 01:11:00.430
Ah, very good. Yes. You know, I do regret

01:11:01.070 --> 01:11:07.070
focusing so much on myself and less on my family. That took a toll after a while. But by God,

01:11:07.070 --> 01:11:11.310
if you're thinking of blowing the whistle, especially on a national security issue,

01:11:11.310 --> 01:11:15.630
hire an attorney first. It's interesting. They do all these terrible things to

01:11:15.630 --> 01:11:20.910
whistleblowers. And whenever you ask them that question, they always say, no, I have no regrets.

01:11:20.910 --> 01:11:25.070
And you know, one of the reasons the prosecutors wanted such a ridiculous sentence

01:11:25.070 --> 01:11:30.430
in the Daniel Hale case was as a deterrent. And you and I were both there when he got up

01:11:30.430 --> 01:11:35.150
and gave that very moving speech. And he mentioned, I don't think any sentence is

01:11:35.150 --> 01:11:39.230
going to be a deterrent because Americans are always going to follow their conscience.

01:11:39.230 --> 01:11:43.230
They're always going to be people when they see torture or children blowing up with drones

01:11:43.310 --> 01:11:49.390
or illegal surveillance who will come forward. There's not really a deterrent in a case like

01:11:49.390 --> 01:11:54.590
this because we do have people like you, like Daniel, like Tom Drake, who follow their conscience.

01:11:54.590 --> 01:11:59.790
And I guess my final question is we're talking in August. This is prerecorded. This is going to

01:11:59.790 --> 01:12:06.430
come out sometime in September. September 11th will be the 20th anniversary of the 2001

01:12:06.430 --> 01:12:12.430
terror attacks. September the 14th will be the anniversary of Congress voting on the AUMF.

01:12:12.430 --> 01:12:18.590
October 7th, I think anniversary of troops in Afghanistan. October 26th anniversary of the

01:12:18.590 --> 01:12:24.190
Patriot Act. 20th anniversary then January, 20th anniversary of First People Guantanamo.

01:12:24.190 --> 01:12:30.190
So we're coming up to 20 years of the war on torture. We invaded Iraq. We invaded Afghanistan.

01:12:30.190 --> 01:12:34.030
We have military operations. And who knows how many countries I don't think the people

01:12:34.030 --> 01:12:38.670
in Congress who are supposed to be overseeing it knows that. We've had warrantless surveillance.

01:12:38.670 --> 01:12:44.430
We've had surveillance with warrants. We've conflated dissent with terrorism. We've tortured

01:12:44.430 --> 01:12:50.350
people. We've carried out war crimes. We've done extradition executions. As someone whose

01:12:50.350 --> 01:12:54.670
background is in counterterrorism, has 20 years of all this made us any safer or any

01:12:54.670 --> 01:13:00.670
better as a country? No. I think it's weakened us as a country. We may not have

01:13:01.230 --> 01:13:07.390
people planning to hijack planes right now and fly them into buildings. But look what we've

01:13:07.390 --> 01:13:13.470
done to civil liberties in our country. I think that we're weaker as a country, weaker as a society

01:13:13.470 --> 01:13:19.550
today compared to 20 years ago. If there were two things that Osama bin Laden set out to do,

01:13:19.550 --> 01:13:25.870
one was to damage the US economy at which he succeeded. And the other was to have Americans

01:13:25.870 --> 01:13:30.190
turn on each other and for us to lose our civil liberties. And he succeeded in that too.

01:13:30.190 --> 01:13:36.590
So no, despite the fact that we spend a trillion dollars a year on our defense budget, despite the

01:13:36.590 --> 01:13:42.270
fact that we spend more money than the next eight largest countries combined, I don't think we're

01:13:42.270 --> 01:13:47.390
safer now than we were 20 years ago. Not at all. John, thank you so much for joining us. It's

01:13:47.390 --> 01:13:49.390
always a pleasure to talk to you.

